ithelial hyperplasia   Columnar cell change   No biopsy site changes are identified   Note  The specimen was radiographed with the calcifications identified in block   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Multiple levels were examined  The specimen was submitted entirely  for microscopic examination   According to the original report   atypical ductal hyperplasia of apocrine  cells  was present  There is a   0 2cm focus of apocrine cysts with a partial  cribriform pattern in 2 of the 9 levels of block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   levels 7 and 8   Note  that level 2 of this block was not sent for review   Diagnostic features of atypical ductal hyperplasia are not seen in the slides  sent for review   Dr  Deborah Dillon reviewed this case and concurs with this interpretation   CLINICAL DATA   History  Cyst and calcifications in the right breast   Clinical Diagnosis  ADH   TISSUE SUBMITTED   Consult slides   GROSS DESCRIPTION   Received from Massachusetts General Hospital  Pathology Department  55 Fruit  Street  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID   are forty vie  45  glass slides   The slides are labeled   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and sublabeled   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID       REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID       REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   through    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    corresponding to A  right breast  subareolar  excision  3 0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE  cm  B  right breast  12 o clock  excision  3 3 x REMOVED_DATE x 1 2cm   performed on  1 2 09  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing the patient s  name   Included is a consultation report from the same hospital numbered   MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and dated